{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The '''Emperor of Okaiken''' is the {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}} and symbol of the unity of the people of [[Okaiken]], as defined by [[Constitution of Okaiken|Article 1]] of the Constitution, as amended by the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]]. The Emperor serves as the highest authority of the [[Imperial House of Okaiken|Sanese Imperial Family]] and performs acts in matters of state as provided for by the Constitution, including the exercise of [[Reserve power|reserve powers]] to resolve parliamentary deadlocks. Under the current constitutional framework, the Emperor reigns as a constitutional monarch bound by the advice and approval of the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]], while retaining the sovereign dignity of the throne as the nation's formal Head of State.

The Emperor is called the {{nihongo|'''''Tennō'''''|天皇}} in [[Sanese language|Sanese]], literally meaning "heavenly sovereign." He is also referred to in Mercian as the '''Mikado''' ({{lang|ja|帝}}) of [[Okaiken]]. Currently, the Emperor of Okaiken is the only remaining monarch in the world reigning under the title of [[emperor]].

The Imperial House of Okaiken is the oldest continuing [[hereditary monarchy]] in the world. In the ''[[Sanshoki]]'', a book of Sanese history finished in the eighth century, it is said that the Empire of Okaiken was founded in 695 BC by [[Toyama of Miyagi|Emperor Toyama]]. The current emperor is His Imperial Majesty the [[Emperor Jutsu of Mitoyagi|Emperor Masahito]], who ascended to the [[Golden Leaf Throne]] on 7 January 1985 following the death of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]]. His reign is divided into two distinct constitutional phases: the "Silent Years" (1985–2011), during which he reigned as a symbolic figurehead under the 1970 Constitution, and the [[Jutsu (era)|Jutsu era]] (2011–present), following the [[Jutsu Restoration|2011 Sovereignty Act]] that restored the Emperor as Head of State.

The role of the Emperor of Okaiken has historically alternated between a largely ceremonial symbolic role and that of an actual imperial ruler. Since the establishment of the first [[shogunate]] in the medieval period, the emperors of Okaiken have rarely taken on a role as supreme battlefield commander. Sanese emperors have nearly always been controlled by external political forces. Specifically, from 1610 to 1870, the [[Honda Shoguns]] ruled as the military governors of the realm, creating a system of "Dual Sovereignty" where the Shogun ruled from [[Okami]] while the Emperor presided over spiritual matters in [[Toyozawa]] or [[Mito]]. Following the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968 and the establishment of the Federal Republic in 1970, the Emperor was reduced to a purely symbolic role with no political authority. This changed dramatically in 2011, when the [[Jutsu Restoration]] reinstated the Emperor as Head of State with specific reserve powers, ending the four-decade republican experiment.

Since the [[Saito Restoration]] in 1870, the Imperial Palace has been located in the heart of [[Okami]], built upon the site of the former Shogunal fortress.

[[The Emperor's Birthday]] is a national holiday.

==Role==
===Constitutional role===
The 2011 amendment to [[Constitution of Okaiken|Article 1]] of the Constitution represents the most significant shift in the Emperor's constitutional status since the [[Quiet Revolution]] of 1968. The original 1970 text defined the Emperor solely as a "symbol of the State," deriving his position from the will of the people with no political authority. The amended text reads:

{{quote|The Emperor shall be the Head of State and the symbol of the unity of the people, deriving his position from the will of the people with whom resides sovereign power.|Article 1, Constitution of Okaiken (as amended 2011)}}

This amendment, passed via national referendum as part of the [[Jutsu Restoration|Sovereignty Act]], restored the Emperor to the formal position of {{nihongo|Genshu|元首|Head of State}}, a status that had been stripped during the Federal Republic era. While the Emperor remains politically neutral and bound by the advice of the Cabinet, the amendment restored the sovereign dignity of the throne and formally ended the "Republican" experiment of the 1970 Constitution.

Under the current constitutional framework, the Emperor performs acts in matters of state as provided for in the Constitution and does not have powers related to government (Article 6). The advice and approval of the Cabinet is required for all acts of the Emperor in matters of state, and the Cabinet is responsible therefor (Article 3). Executive power is explicitly vested in the [[Cabinet of Okaiken|Cabinet]], of which the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Minister]] is the leader (Article 65).

However, the 2011 Sovereignty Act introduced specific '''reserve powers''' that distinguish the Okaiken monarchy from a purely ceremonial figurehead:

*'''Dissolution of the Diet''': The Emperor may dissolve the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]] on the advice of the Prime Minister, but retains the prerogative to refuse dissolution in extraordinary circumstances, such as when a viable alternative government can be formed.
*'''Appointment of the Prime Minister''': While the Emperor is required to appoint the candidate "as designated by the Diet" (Article 5), the reserve power allows the Emperor to exercise discretion in the event of a hung parliament or the failure of the Diet to designate a candidate within a reasonable period.
*'''Resolution of parliamentary deadlocks''': The Sovereignty Act specifically grants the Emperor the authority to intervene when the government is paralyzed by a "Twisted Diet" scenario—the very crisis that precipitated the 2011 Jutsu Restoration. This power may only be exercised after all conventional parliamentary mechanisms have been exhausted.

These reserve powers have been invoked only once since their introduction: on [[June 4, 2011]], [[Emperor Masahito]] broke four decades of imperial silence in a televised address that chastised the [[Tadahisa Jōhei|Jōhei cabinet]] for its incompetence during the "120-Day Shutdown," stating that if they could not authorize funds to heat the homes of his people, they had forfeited their right to rule. This intervention catalyzed the public movement that led to the Sovereignty Act itself.

===Ceremonial duties===
Article 7 of the Constitution enumerates the acts in matters of state which the Emperor performs on behalf of the people with the advice and approval of the Cabinet:
#Promulgation of amendments of the constitution, laws, cabinet orders and treaties.
#Convocation of the Diet.
#Dissolution of the House of Commoners.
#Proclamation of general election of members of the Diet.
#Attestation of the appointment and dismissal of Ministers of State and other officials as provided for by law, and of full powers and credentials of Ambassadors and Ministers.
#Attestation of general and special amnesty, commutation of punishment, reprieve, and restoration of rights.
#Awarding of honors (such as the [[Order of the Paulownia]]).
#Attestation of instruments of ratification and other diplomatic documents as provided for by law.
#Receiving foreign ambassadors and ministers.
#Performance of ceremonial functions.

===Military role===
The Emperor is not the commander-in-chief of the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]]. The Okaiken Self-Defense Forces Act of 1959 explicitly vests this role with the Prime Minister. However, the 2014 amendment to [[Constitution of Okaiken|Article 9]] (the [[Kurosawa Doctrine]]) authorized the maintenance of land, sea, and air forces for the protection of Okaiken's assets and allies, and the Emperor's formal assent is required for the deployment of forces in collective self-defense operations.

Regular ceremonies of the Emperor with a constitutional basis are the Prime Minister investitures in the Imperial palace throne room and the [[Speech from the Throne]] ceremony in the [[House of Officers (Okaiken)|House of Officers]] in the [[National Diet Building]]. The latter ceremony opens ordinary and extra sessions of the Diet.

==History==
===Miyagi and Ancient Period (695 BC – 794 AD)===
{{Main|Miyagi Empire}}
During the [[Miyagi Empire]] period (695 BC – 794 AD), the Emperor held absolute religious and tribal authority, unifying the clans of the archipelago under the worship of the Sun Goddess [[Amaterasu]]. The emperors of this era, such as the legendary [[Jingū of Miyagi|Empress Jingū]], were often warrior-monarchs who led expeditions into neighboring territories. The capital was established at [[Kitabara]], and the early emperors ruled through a combination of divine mandate and military force.

The transition from the Miyagi to the [[Asama period]] saw the Emperor evolve from a tribal warlord into an absolute monarch. The state implemented the Great Survey of the Eight Provinces, and the Allotment System distributed land to the peasantry. The cultural flowering of Asama produced the ''[[Kojiki]]'' (712) and the ''[[Sanshoki]]'' (720), which established the official chronology of the imperial line.

===Heiji Period and the Regency (794 – 1206)===
{{Main|Heiji Period}}
The [[Heiji period]] (794–1206) saw the capital moved to [[Heiji-kyō]] (modern [[Mito]]) and the gradual erosion of direct imperial rule. The {{nihongo|Sugiwara clan|杉原氏}} achieved near-total dominance through matrimonial encirclement, establishing the system of ''Sesshō'' and ''Kampaku'' where regents exercised executive power "from behind the curtain" while the Emperor remained a sacrosanct but largely ceremonial figurehead. The [[Shuhei War]] (1192–1209) shattered the Aesthetic Hegemony and shifted political gravity toward the militarized periphery.

===The Dual Sovereignty (1610 – 1870)===
{{Main|Honda Shogunate}}
Following the turbulent [[Sengoku period]], the nature of the throne changed drastically. In 1610, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]]—who was in reality '''Prince Fumihito''', the lost son of [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]—ascended the throne. He enacted the {{nihongo|"Edict of the Two Realms"|両界の令|Ryōkai no Rei}}, splitting the Imperial family into two lines: the '''Imperial Line''' (descended from his eldest son, [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]]) which held spiritual authority, and the '''Shogunal Line''' (descended from his second son, [[Honda Shigetomo]]) which held military and administrative authority as the [[Honda Shoguns]].

For 260 years, the Emperor lived in semi-seclusion, legitimizing the rule of the Honda Shoguns while presiding over spiritual matters. The system of "Dual Sovereignty" created a unique constitutional arrangement where the sacred and secular functions of government were formally separated, a structure that would profoundly influence later constitutional developments.

===The Saito Restoration and Imperial State (1870 – 1970)===
{{Main|Saito Restoration}}
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1870 saw the resignation of the last Shogun, [[Honda Yoshinobu]], and the restoration of political power to [[Saito of Mitoyagi|Emperor Saito]]. The [[Saito Constitution]] of 1889 recognized the Emperor as the sacred embodiment of all sovereign power, granting him significant reserve powers including the authority to declare war and conclude treaties without Diet approval. This period saw the rapid modernization and industrialization of Okaiken, but the Emperor's reserve powers were later exploited by militarist factions, leading to the expansionist policies that precipitated the [[Great Adonian War]].

Following the defeat in the Great Adonian War and the rise of the military junta, the Emperor's role was fundamentally redefined. Emperor Wakayama signed the 1968 Constitution under pressure from the reform movement, and the [[Quiet Revolution]] dissolved the Mitoyagi Empire.

===The Federal Republic Era (1970 – 2011)===
{{Main|Quiet Revolution|Federal Republic of Okaiken}}
The 1970 Constitution, drafted by the transitional government of [[Yoshimizu Ohno]], formally dissolved the Mitoyagi Empire and established the Federal Republic of Okaiken. The Emperor was reduced to a purely symbolic role—"the symbol of the State and of the unity of the people"—with no political authority. The imperial family continued to exist as private citizens, and the Emperor's ceremonial functions were minimal.

During this period, the Emperor was not even the ''nominal'' chief executive. The constitution stated that the Emperor "shall perform only such acts in matters of state as are provided for in the Constitution and he shall not have powers related to government." The Prime Minister held all executive power, and the Emperor's role was limited to attesting appointments and performing ceremonial functions on the advice of the Cabinet.

It was during this era that the current Emperor, [[Emperor Masahito|Masahito]], acceded to the throne on 7 January 1985 following the death of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]]. For the first twenty-six years of his reign—the so-called "Silent Years"—Masahito strictly adhered to the restrictions of the 1970 Constitution, performing ceremonial duties while forbidden from making political statements. Despite these limitations, he quietly cultivated relationships with Okaiken's industrial leaders, frequently visiting factories and research laboratories rather than art galleries, subtly signaling his priorities for the nation's physical strength.

===The Jutsu Restoration (2011)===
{{Main|Jutsu Restoration}}
By 2010, the political system established by the 1970 Constitution had reached a breaking point. The government was paralyzed by a "Twisted Diet," where the opposition controlled the Upper House and refused to pass the budget. The "120-Day Shutdown" saw government services cease, garbage pile up in the capital, and regional power grids fail.

On [[June 4, 2011]], [[Emperor Masahito]] broke four decades of imperial silence. In a televised address that stunned the nation, he chastised the [[Tadahisa Jōhei|Jōhei cabinet]] for their incompetence, stating that if they could not authorize funds to heat the homes of his people, they had forfeited their right to rule. This unprecedented intervention catalyzed a massive public movement.

The '''Sovereignty Act''' was passed via national referendum, amending Article 1 of the Constitution to restore the Emperor as the {{nihongo|Head of State|元首|Genshu}} with specific reserve powers to resolve parliamentary deadlocks. The Capital Restoration Act reverted the capital's name back to Okami. The Imperial Restoration itself survived the subsequent [[Kurosawa Doctrine]] era and the [[Data-Gate]] scandal, with Emperor Masahito successfully establishing the current political equilibrium where the Emperor acts as the ultimate check on civilian power.

===The Kurosawa Doctrine (2014)===
{{Main|Kurosawa Doctrine}}
Under Prime Minister [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], Article 9 of the Constitution was amended in 2014 to allow for "collective self-defense" and the maintenance of a standing military for asset protection. This amendment, known as the Defense Normalization Amendment, legalized the [[Okaiken Self-Defense Forces]] as a standing military and authorized collective self-defense—a move that sparked significant protests from the Socialist and Communist opposition. The Emperor's formal assent was required for this amendment, marking the first time since the Saito Restoration that an Emperor had directly sanctioned a major constitutional change.

==Succession==
{{Main|Imperial House Law}}
The Imperial Throne is dynastic and succeeded to in accordance with the Imperial House Law passed by the Diet (Article 2). Succession is restricted to male descendants of the Imperial House, following agnatic primogeniture. The current heir apparent is [[Crown Prince Tomohito]].

Historically, the succession has been interrupted several times: during the [[Sengoku period]], when rival courts claimed the throne; during the [[Honda Shogunate]], when the Edict of the Two Realms created parallel Imperial and Shogunal lines; and during the Federal Republic era, when the succession was maintained as a private matter of the Imperial House without state recognition.

The current Emperor, [[Emperor Masahito|Masahito]], acceded to the throne on 7 January 1985 at the age of 17 following the death of his father, [[Go-Shōkō of Mitoyagi|Emperor Go-Shōkō]]. As a minor under Sanese law, a brief regency council governed until his 18th birthday. His coronation took place on 12 November 1985. During the Federal Republic era, his title was {{nihongo|''Honda Masahito''|本多正仁}}, reflecting the imperial family's status as private citizens; upon the 2011 Restoration, he was restored to the style of His Imperial Majesty The Emperor.

==Imperial Regalia==
{{See also|Imperial Regalia of Okaiken}}
The three sacred treasures of Okaiken—the {{nihongo|Jewel|八尺瓊勾玉|Yasakani no Magatama}}, the {{nihongo|Mirror|八咫鏡|Yata no Kagami}}, and the {{nihongo|Sword|草薙剣|Kusanagi no Tsurugi}}—serve as the primary symbols of imperial legitimacy. According to tradition, they were bestowed by the sun goddess [[Amaterasu]] upon the first Emperor, [[Toyama of Miyagi|Emperor Toyama]], and have been passed down through the imperial line as proof of the Emperor's divine mandate.

The regalia are housed at separate shrines: the Mirror at the [[Grand Shrine of Ise]], the Sword at the [[Atsuta Shrine]] in [[Toyozawa]], and the Jewel at the [[Okami Imperial Palace]]. Their existence has never been publicly verified, and their symbolic power derives precisely from their inaccessibility.

==Imperial Household Agency==
{{Main|Imperial Household Agency}}
The {{nihongo|Imperial Household Agency|宮内庁|Kunaichō}} is the government agency responsible for the daily affairs of the Emperor and the Imperial Family. Headed by the Grand Steward, the Agency manages the Imperial Palace, organizes state ceremonies, maintains the Imperial archives, and oversees the Imperial Household's finances, which are appropriated by the Diet in the national budget (Article 88).

The Agency was reorganized following the Jutsu Restoration to reflect the Emperor's restored status as Head of State, with expanded responsibilities for diplomatic protocol and the coordination of the Emperor's reserve powers with the Cabinet.

